EXT. BRIDGE OF BONES OVER THE GREY GORGE - DAWN

A bridge strung between two cliffs, built from the ribs of some long-dead leviathan. Fog fills the gorge below. Mountain wind pulls at cloaks.

A party of four stands at the near end.

AELFWYN THORNBURY (30s, ranger, bow across her back, scar through one eyebrow) tests the first rib with her boot.

GRIMBALD "GRIM" OKORO-SHALE (50s, dwarf-born, braided beard, iron-shod staff) leans on the staff and glares at the fog.

THISTLE-WREN (late teens, half-fey, hair like dandelion down) hops from foot to foot, humming.

BROTHER CASSIAN VELLACOTT (40s, wandering monk, wooden prayer beads, travel-worn robe) kneels briefly, touches the stone, rises.

AELFWYN
(to the group)
We cross single file. Spacing of three paces. Anyone who stumbles, the others hold.

GRIM
(muttering)
Three paces. As if the fog will respect it.

THISTLE-WREN
It will! I asked it nicely!

AELFWYN
Thistle, you don't ask fog.

THISTLE-WREN
That's why it listens.

CASSIAN
(quietly, to Aelfwyn)
The ribs are old. Older than the oldest prayer I know.

AELFWYN
Will they hold?

CASSIAN
They have so far.

Aelfwyn nods. Steps out onto the bridge. The bone creaks, then settles. She takes three paces and stops. Grim follows. Thistle-Wren follows Grim, humming louder. Cassian takes the rear, counting his beads.

Halfway across, a gust slams the bridge sideways. The party freezes. Thistle-Wren grabs Grim's belt. Grim drops his staff to the rib to steady himself. Aelfwyn kneels, one hand on the bone, the other signaling Cassian to hold. The fog thickens until none of them can see more than an arm's length ahead.

GRIM
(to himself)
Idiot dwarf. Never trust a bridge made of something that died.

CASSIAN
(calling)
Everyone, breathe. Let the wind pass through you.

THISTLE-WREN
I'm trying! My lungs are very small!

AELFWYN
Quiet, all of you. Listen.

They listen. Far below, in the white, something large exhales.

THISTLE-WREN
(whispering)
It's the leviathan.

GRIM
It's dead.

THISTLE-WREN
Its ghost isn't.

CASSIAN
(softly)
Then we cross as guests.

The wind drops. The fog parts for a moment, revealing the far side: a cairn, a green slope, a thin road winding into pines.

AELFWYN
Move. Now. Quick and quiet.

They run, three paces apart, bone ringing under their boots like a xylophone of the dead. Thistle-Wren giggles. Grim wheezes. Cassian's beads clack. Aelfwyn counts, lips tight.

They reach the far cliff and tumble onto grass. Grim lies flat, staring at the sky.

GRIM
(to the sky)
Never again.

THISTLE-WREN
We have to cross back.

GRIM
(to himself)
I'll sleep here.

Behind them, the bridge sighs and sways. The fog closes over it like a lid.

CASSIAN
(to the group)
We should keep moving. The road is long.

AELFWYN
(offering her hand to Grim)
Up, old friend.

He takes it. She hauls him upright. The four stand in a row, looking down the road.

THISTLE-WREN
Where does it go?

AELFWYN
Wherever we go.

They start walking. The wind follows at their heels, gentle now, almost apologetic.
